The most common JMM bug is the **stale flag problem**. Thread A writes a boolean, but Thread B never sees the update because the JIT compiler hoists the read out of the loop (it sees that `running` is never written *within* the loop body, so it caches it in a register):

```java
// BUG: Without volatile, the JIT may optimize this to while(true){}
// because it sees 'running' is never modified inside this method's scope
class UnsafeFlag {
    boolean running = true;         // not volatile — JIT can cache this
    void stop() { running = false; } // this write may stay in CPU cache
    void loop() { while (running) {} } // JIT hoists read → infinite loop
}

// FIX: volatile forces every read/write to go through main memory
class SafeFlag {
    volatile boolean running = true;  // volatile = read/write from main memory
    void stop()  { running = false; } // write is immediately visible to all cores
    void loop()  { while (running) {} } // each iteration re-reads from memory
}
```

The **double-checked locking** pattern is the classic interview question for JMM. The problem is subtle: `instance = new Singleton()` is not a single operation — it involves (1) allocating memory, (2) running the constructor to initialize fields, and (3) assigning the reference to `instance`. The JVM can reorder steps 2 and 3, meaning another thread can see a non-null `instance` whose fields haven't been initialized yet:

```java
// BROKEN: Thread B can see instance != null before constructor completes
class BrokenSingleton {
    private static BrokenSingleton instance; // NOT volatile
    public static BrokenSingleton get() {
        if (instance == null) {                       // Thread B: sees non-null
            synchronized (BrokenSingleton.class) {
                if (instance == null) {
                    instance = new BrokenSingleton();  // Thread A: steps 2 & 3 reordered
                }
            }
        }
        return instance; // Thread B: gets a half-constructed object!
    }
}

// FIX: volatile prevents reordering of the constructor writes
// The volatile write of 'instance' happens-after ALL constructor writes
class SafeSingleton {
    private static volatile SafeSingleton instance;  // volatile fixes it
    public static SafeSingleton get() {
        SafeSingleton local = instance;  // single volatile read (optimization)
        if (local == null) {
            synchronized (SafeSingleton.class) {
                local = instance;
                if (local == null) {
                    instance = local = new SafeSingleton();
                }
            }
        }
        return local;
    }
}
```

The **simplest and recommended** approach avoids volatile entirely by leveraging the JVM's guarantee that a class is initialized exactly once, atomically, the first time it's accessed:

```java
// BEST: JVM guarantees class loading is thread-safe and lazy
class BestSingleton {
    private BestSingleton() {}
    // Holder class is not loaded until get() is called for the first time
    // JVM's class initialization lock ensures thread safety — no volatile needed
    private static class Holder {
        static final BestSingleton INSTANCE = new BestSingleton();
    }
    public static BestSingleton get() { return Holder.INSTANCE; }
}
```

This **initialization-on-demand holder** pattern is what you should recommend in interviews — it's lazy, thread-safe, and requires no synchronization keywords.

Understanding common GC-related patterns helps you write code that cooperates well with the garbage collector and avoid the most frequent causes of memory leaks.

**The #1 memory leak pattern** is a static collection that grows forever. Since static fields are GC roots, any object stored in them stays reachable and will never be collected:

```java
// BUG: static list is a GC root — entries are NEVER collected
// This is the most common cause of Java OOM in long-running services
static final List<byte[]> LEAK = new ArrayList<>();
static void leakExample() {
    LEAK.add(new byte[1024 * 1024]); // 1MB added, never removed
    // After 1000 calls: 1GB of unreclaimable heap
}
```

**Soft and Weak references** give you GC-cooperative caching. A `SoftReference` is kept alive until the JVM is under memory pressure — making it ideal for caches that should shrink under load rather than cause OOM:

```java
// SoftReference: GC keeps this alive as long as memory is plentiful
// When heap gets tight, GC reclaims it — your code handles the miss
SoftReference<byte[]> cache = new SoftReference<>(loadExpensiveData());
byte[] data = cache.get();   // returns null if GC reclaimed it
if (data == null) {
    data = loadExpensiveData(); // cache miss — reload
}
```

A `WeakReference` (and `WeakHashMap`) is collected at the **next GC cycle** regardless of memory pressure. This is useful for metadata maps where the entry should disappear when the key is no longer used:

```java
// WeakHashMap: entries are automatically removed when the key
// is no longer strongly referenced anywhere else
var metadata = new WeakHashMap<Object, String>();
Object key = new Object();
metadata.put(key, "session-info");
key = null;                     // key is now only weakly reachable
// After next GC: metadata.size() → 0 (entry auto-removed)
```

Finally, remember that GC handles **memory** but not native resources like file handles, sockets, or database connections. Always use **try-with-resources** for deterministic cleanup:

```java
// try-with-resources calls close() at block exit — guaranteed,
// regardless of exceptions, and independent of when GC runs
try (var stream = new FileInputStream("/tmp/data.txt");
     var reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(stream))) {
    return reader.readLine();
}   // both reader and stream are closed here, even if readLine() throws
```
